Nico (support): Heads-up — customer BlueThera hit a reproducible case where an SSE stream is terminated mid-response for quota reasons, the client reconnects immediately, and they see overlapping token charges on their invoice. They provided a small browser fetch snippet and edge traces. Filing to triage. :(

Harper (eng-runtime): Thanks. Drop repro + any req ids. Do they reuse the same auth key/session header on reconnect?

Nico (support): They do reuse same API key and send an `X-Client-Session` header. Minimal repro (browser fetch) they shared (sanitized):

```js
// naive browser SSE reconnect
async function startStream() {
  const headers = { Authorization: 'Bearer SK-BT-XXX', Accept: 'text/event-stream', 'X-Client-Session': 'bt-77' };
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.example/v1/streams?model=gpt-4o-mini', { headers });
  if (r.status !== 200) throw new Error('bad status ' + r.status);
  const reader = r.body.getReader();
  try {
    while (true) {
      const { value, done } = await reader.read();
      if (done) break;
      // on any stream error the client immediately re-calls startStream()
      if (detectQuotaEvent(value)) startStream();
    }
  } catch (e) {
    // immediate reconnect on any exception (no backoff)
    startStream();
  }
}
startStream();
```

Edge log (redacted):
[edge] 2026-12-01T12:02:05Z req=req-A1 path=/v1/streams emitted_tokens=142 upstream=200
[edge] 2026-12-01T12:02:14Z req=req-A1 sse_event=quota_close reason="quota_exceeded"
[edge] 2026-12-01T12:02:14Z req=req-A1 connection=terminated
[edge] 2026-12-01T12:02:15Z req=req-A2 reopened_by=client session=bt-77 emitted_tokens=95

Customer says billing shows charges corresponding to both req-A1 and req-A2 and believes the overlap shouldn't be billed twice. They want a fix or a credit.

Zoe (sre): Quick summary of the infra behavior: our runtime records emitted tokens to billing incrementally as we flush SSE chunks (to keep latency low). We also have a separate quota enforcement that can cancel the stream mid-flight when session consumption surpasses limits. That causes the connection to close and a customer client to reconnect. Billing currently treats req-A1 and req-A2 as independent and bills both.

Harper (eng-runtime): Two possible fixes: 1) Make server return an HTTP 429 + SSE `event: throttled` with a `retry_after` field so well-behaved clients back off instead of immediately retrying. 2) Add short-lived dedupe at billing keyed by (`X-Client-Session` or `X-Stream-Session`, request fingerprint) for ~20s window so reconnects reuse the same session won't double-charge. Longer-term: implement provisional reservation of tokens until explicit stream close or inactivity timeout.

Nico (support): They attached the raw HTTP response snippet when quota tripped:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/event-stream
X-Request-Id: req-A1

SSE payload lines:
event: quota_close
data: {"reason":"quota_exceeded","retry_after":4}

Note: we returned 200 with SSE event instead of 429. Client app immediately reopened because it treats connection close as implicit retry signal.

Lina (billing): If we are recording tokens on flush, the ledger entries are created for req-A1 when those tokens emit. We could post-process and mark duplicates during a small window if session header matches. For emergency customer relief we can issue a manual credit after we confirm the two reqs are within the same session and overlap is suspected. We need both request ids and timestamps to do the query.

Zoe (sre): I'm going to push two instrumentation changes now: emit `session_key` into structured edge logs when `X-Client-Session` present, and add a counter `streams.quota_closures.midstream` with labels for model/tenant. This will help quantify incidence. Pushing in next hour.

Harper (eng-runtime): I'll prototype a small change to send an `X-Stream-Session` on initial stream response (customer-supplied session id echoed back or server-generated) and also include an `http_status: 429` when we intend for clients to backoff. That makes client behavior clearer. ETA: patch + canary in ~48h.

rate-limit-bot: Alert: canary rollout "sse-retry-after-429" started on 5% of edge nodes.

Lina (billing): For this specific tenant we can do a one-off credit if support verifies req ids. Nico, please gather req-A1 and req-A2 full traces and confirm. We'll also document the credit criteria in playbook.

Nico (support): Got the req ids confirmed and passed to billing. Customer applied a quick client change to add a 3-5s sleep before reconnecting and reported their next test did not show duplicate charges. They asked whether Redwood will change server behavior — please keep me posted so I can communicate timeline.

Harper (eng-runtime): Follow-up action items: 
- Harper: Add `Retry-After` + switch to 429 for mid-stream quota closures, and return `X-Stream-Session` header on stream start. (ETA 48h canary)
- Zoe: Add metrics for mid-stream quota closures and enrich logs with session_key. (ETA 1h)
- Lina: Credit playbook entry and short dedupe logic proposal for billing team. (ETA 2d)
- Nico: Collect req traces and confirm credit applied. (ETA today)

Zoe (sre): Note: we should also update docs and SDK samples: clients must honor `Retry-After` and not immediately reconnect on stream close. We'll add an SSE patterns page (best-effort reconnect/backoff, reuse session header).

Nico (support): Customer accepted the provisional credit and patched client. They said stable now on their tests. I'll close the ticket once canary is live. Thanks all. :white_check_mark:

-- end of thread --
